eficiency state (a genotype cause) rather than a disease. 'Deficiency' is a general term that is excluded, and the disease under study is hemochromatosis.\",\"guideline_section\":\"What NOT to Annotate 4: Do not annotate general terms\"},{\"id\":3,\"entity\":\"SpecificDisease\",\"rationale\":\"'genetic hemochromatosis' refers to one specific form of hemochromatosis (HFE-related hereditary iron overload). The whole phrase is the most specific span, so it is annotated as one Specific Disease.\",\"guideline_section\":\"What to Annotate 5: Annotate minimum necessary span of text\"},{\"id\":4,\"entity\":\"NONE\",\"rationale\":\"'HFE-deficient' describes the genotype of the experimental animals, not a named disease. It modifies 'animals' and is a genetic-state descriptor, so it is not annotated as a disease mention.\",\"guideline_section\":\"What NOT to Annotate 4: Do not annotate general terms\"},{\"id\":5,\"entity\":\"SpecificDisease\",\"rationale\":\"'hemochromatosis' in 'mimicking human hemochromatosis' is the same specific disease. 'Human' is an organism modifier and is excluded from the span.\",\"guideline_section\":\"What to Annotate 1: Annotate all Specific Disease mentions\"},{\"id\":6,\"entity\":\"SpecificDisease\",\"rationale\":\"'iron overload' names a defined pathological condition (a disease-category concept) that the mice develop. It is not a general term like 'complications' and is treated as a specific disease mention.\",\"guideline_section\":\"What to Annotate 1: Annotate all Specific Disease mentions\"}]}", "type": "text"}], "diagnostics": null, "model": "claude-haiku-5-5", "role": "assistant", "stop_details": null, "stop_reason": "end_turn", "stop_sequence": null, "type": "message", "usage": {"cache_creation": {"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens": 0, "ephemeral_5m_input_tokens": 0}, "cache_creation_input_tokens": 0, "cache_read_input_tokens": 0, "inference_geo": "global", "input_tokens": 4359, "output_tokens": 2667, "output_tokens_details": {"thinking_tokens":